# Chapter 8 · The Development Theories Compared: A Critical Gender Analysis

> "Give a critical gender analysis of development theories" (2024). This chapter brings Chapters 4–7 together and adds the feminist and post-development critiques, so that you can answer a question on the theories as a group.

## 8.1  What a "critical gender analysis" means

A **gender analysis** of a theory asks: what does it say about **women and men**; what does it **assume**; what does it **leave out**; and what would its **policies** do to gender relations? A **critical** analysis also judges its **strengths and weaknesses** and asks **whose interests** it serves.

::: fig grid | Five questions for a critical gender analysis of any development theory.
@cols 5
Cause: why are people poor?
Women: where do they appear?
Assumptions: about family and work
Silences: what is left out?
Policies: effect on gender relations
:::

## 8.2  The master table

::: table | The four development theories — a critical gender analysis.
@widths 2.6,3,3,2.8,2.6
Theory | Cause of poverty | View of women | Main gender critique | Gender-aware use today
Modernisation (Rostow 1960) | Internal "tradition" | Freed by modernity; mothers and homemakers | Blames culture; ignores structures; men captured benefits (Boserup) | Education, urbanisation and media do widen choices
Dependency (Frank 1966) | External exploitation by the centre | Largely absent | Gender-blind; ignores local patriarchy and unpaid work | Explains cheap female labour and debt
World-system (Wallerstein 1974) | A single unequal world economy | Absent at first; later households and the global assembly line | Early gender blindness; women as victims | Gendered globalisation, care chains
Structural functionalism (Parsons 1955) | Incomplete modernisation | Expressive role in the home | Justifies inequality; ignores power and women's work | Institutions and socialisation
:::

## 8.3  Five shared weaknesses

1. **Gender blindness.** All four theories began by treating "society", "the economy" or "the nation" as gender-neutral.
2. **Invisibility of unpaid work.** None counted the **unpaid care and subsistence work** on which economies rest.
3. **The household as a unit.** All assumed the household is a harmonious unit that shares income fairly. Feminist economists (**Amartya Sen**'s "cooperative conflict", ==1990==; **Bina Agarwal**, ==1997==) showed households are sites of **bargaining** in which women often lose.
4. **Women as passive.** Women appear as victims of tradition (modernisation), of global capital (dependency, world-system) or as keepers of the home (functionalism) — rarely as **actors**.
5. **Male-centred models of progress.** Progress was measured by male-dominated sectors — industry, cash crops, formal jobs.

## 8.4  The feminist responses

The critique of these theories produced the **three approaches** of the next chapters:

::: fig flow | From development theories to gender approaches.
@per 3
Modernisation's blind spot: women left out
WID (1970s): integrate women into development
Dependency's and Marxism's insight: exploitation
WAD (late 1970s): women already integrated, unequally
Feminist theory of power relations
GAD (1980s): transform gender relations
:::

- **WID** answered modernisation: "include women" (Chapter 9).
- **WAD** drew on dependency and Marxist theory: "women are exploited within the system" (Chapter 10).
- **GAD** drew on socialist feminism: "change **gender relations** of power" (Chapter 11).

## 8.5  Later critiques

- **Human development and capabilities** (Haq, Sen, **Martha Nussbaum**, *Women and Human Development*, ==2000==): judge development by people's real freedoms, including women's.
- **Post-development and decolonial** critiques (Escobar, ==1995==; Mohanty, ==1984==; Lugones, ==2007==): development itself can reproduce colonial power (Chapter 3).
- **Feminist economics** (Marilyn Waring, Diane Elson, Nancy Folbre): count and value **care**.
- **Ecofeminism and feminist environmentalism** (Chapter 14): development that destroys nature harms women first.

::: fig spectrum | Where the theories place the cause of women's disadvantage.
@left Inside the society (culture, family)
@right Outside the society (global system)
10: Modernisation — tradition and culture
30: Structural functionalism — family roles
72: Dependency — the centre exploits the periphery
90: World-system — one unequal world economy
:::

## 8.5a  What each theory would recommend for women — and what a gender analysis adds

::: table | Policy implications of each theory for women.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
Theory | Its recommendation | What a gender analysis adds
Modernisation | Educate women; spread modern values; family planning | Change land, credit and inheritance too; count unpaid work
Dependency | Break unequal ties; national self-reliance | Change local patriarchy; value women's subsistence work
World-system | Rise from periphery to semi-periphery | Protect women workers in export chains; care-chain rights
Structural functionalism | Support stable family roles | Treat roles as changeable; share care between men and women
:::

A gender analysis therefore does not reject these theories; it **completes** them by asking about women's work, property, time and power in each case.

## 8.6  A balanced conclusion

Each theory captures part of the truth. **Culture** matters (modernisation); **institutions** shape roles (functionalism); **global structures** exploit poor countries (dependency, world-system). What all lacked was attention to **gender relations of power** — inside households, markets and states. A critical gender analysis joins the **local** and the **global**, the **cultural** and the **structural**, and adds **unpaid work** and **women's agency**.

::: pakistan | Applying the theories to one Pakistani woman
A woman cotton picker in southern Punjab:
- **Modernisation** would say her poverty comes from tradition and lack of education.
- **Functionalism** would treat her as a homemaker who "helps" in the fields.
- **Dependency** would point to low world cotton prices and Pakistan's weak position in trade.
- **World-system** would place her at the bottom of a global textile chain whose profits flow to the core.
- **A gender analysis** adds: she does most of the family's unpaid care; she owns no land; her wages are paid to her husband; she has no labour protection — and she may be organising with other women for better rates.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2024
1. Introduction: what "critical gender analysis" means; the five questions. 2–5. One heading per theory — claims, view of women, critique. 6. Comparative table. 7. Shared weaknesses. 8. Feminist responses (WID, WAD, GAD) and later critiques. 9. Pakistan (the cotton picker). 10. Conclusion: join the local and global. Draw the spectrum or the table.
:::
